# Let a KV connector (LMCache MP, NIXL, ...) run against an emulated vLLM.
#
# Emulation replaces the forward pass, and both halves of that replacement
# break a KV connector:
#
#   1. cuda_mock's initialize_from_config stub allocates nothing, so
#      ensure_kv_transfer_initialized() never runs (no worker-side connector
#      exists) and register_kv_caches() is never called (no buffers to give
#      it).
#   2. The executor hook short-circuits before GPUModelRunner.execute_model,
#      which is where vLLM drives the worker side of the connector
#      (KVConnectorModelRunnerMixin._get_kv_connector_output:
#      bind_connector_metadata / start_load_kv / wait_for_save /
#      get_finished).  ModelRunnerOutput.kv_connector_output therefore stays
#      None: the scheduler never learns an async load finished, so requests
#      park in WAITING_FOR_REMOTE_KVS forever, and nothing is ever stored, so
#      the cache stays empty and no request ever hits.
#
# Both are fixed here, and both are inert without --kv-transfer-config, so a
# connector-free emulation run is unchanged.
#
# 1. cuda_mock._synthesize_kv_caches / _maybe_init_kv_connector
#    A connector does not care that KV buffers live in VRAM; it cares about
#    their shape, dtype and byte count, all of which vLLM has already computed
#    and put in the KVCacheConfig.  So allocate them in HOST memory from that
#    config and register those.  Contents are zeros; volumes are real, which
#    is what makes the measured transfer cost real.
#
#    Note this must NOT go through GPUModelRunner.initialize_kv_cache(): its
#    bind_kv_cache() resolves layer names against
#    compilation_config.static_forward_context, which is populated by Attention
#    module construction during load_model -- stubbed out here, so that dict is
#    empty and the real path raises.
#
#    LMCache needs no changes to accept host buffers: with DEVICE_TYPE=cpu
#    (see patches/lmcache/10-*.patch) its device detection resolves to
#    StubCPUDevice, and lmcache/v1/platform/cpu/shm.py migrates a CPU tensor's
#    storage into a POSIX SHM segment the mp server can map -- the same
#    server-pull topology as HIP IPC, minus the GPU.  The emulator container
#    needs `ipc: host` for that, since Docker isolates /dev/shm per container.
#
#    Sizing: these are the full pool vLLM asked for, which on a real MI300X
#    pack is the captured available_kv_cache_bytes (~100+ GiB).  torch.zeros on
#    CPU is lazily backed, so untouched blocks cost no resident memory;
#    VLLM_EMULATOR_MEMORY shrinks the emulated pool -- and the emulated cliff --
#    to fit the node, and VLLM_EMULATOR_KV_BUFFERS=0 opts out entirely.
#
# 2. executor_hook._kv_connector_step
#    Reproduces the mixin's lifecycle around the emulated forward, and
#    attaches the resulting KVConnectorOutput to the fake ModelRunnerOutput.
#    should_use_oracle() now also fires on a zero-token batch that has
#    connector metadata -- vLLM's kv_connector_no_forward() case, where every
#    request is parked on an async load and only a connector poll can un-park
#    them.  Skipping it is a deadlock, not an optimisation.
#
#    Known fidelity gap, documented in the code: the cycle runs synchronously
#    on the engine thread (where vLLM runs it) rather than inside the
#    threading.Timer modelling emulated compute time, so a store is submitted
#    up to one step early.  Fine for hit rate, transfer volume and throughput;
#    wrong for tight per-step store-latency attribution.
#
# 3. _stub_get_kv_cache_spec built FullAttentionSpec with model_config.dtype.
#    The KV pool is sized in the CACHE dtype: --kv-cache-dtype fp8 reported
#    bf16-sized pages, i.e. 2x page_size_bytes and 2x too few blocks for the
#    same budget.  That moved the emulated KV-admission cliff on its own, and
#    would now also missize every registered buffer.
#
